model like\n195:   any worker's with `swarm{action:\"model\", name:<the narrator's member name>,\n196:   model:…}`.\n197: \n198: ## The monitor\n199: \n200: This applies only if you added a monitor (**Start it**, step 4). The monitor\n201: (`monitor`, or `monitor-N`) wakes when the whole swarm has gone quiet and tells\n202: whoever can move it. Do not brief it, assign it work, or kill it unless the user\n203: asks.\n204: \n205: - **`@coordinator [stall]` means nobody is coming.** Act on it this turn: read\n206:   the `#seq`s it cites, then hand out the next step by name, switch the model of\n207:   a worker whose turn failed (`swarm{action:\"model\"}`), or re-post a result that\n208:   went to nobody to the right `@name`.\n209: - **Do not reply to the monitor** on the board.\n210: - **After a server restart** (a notice that your subagents and background tasks\n211:   were stopped), post `@<the monitor's member name> resume`: the restart\n212:   stopped its timer, and only a post naming it wakes it to start a new one.\n213: - **Say when you are done.** When you report the finished job to the user, first\n214:   post one line `[done] <summary>` with no `@` in it, so the monitor goes quiet.\n215: \n216: ## Shape it\n217: \n218: - **Verifiers.** Give the swarm workers whose only job is to check the others'\n219:   work — read the diff, run the tests, try the thing as a user would — and post\n220:   what is wrong, by name, to whoever wrote it. Aim for one verifier for every\n221:   two to four workers. A verifier reports and the author fixes; a verifier that\n222:   fixes things itself puts two workers in the same file.\n223: - **Do not be the bottleneck.** If workers sit waiting while you divide up the\n224:   work, hand that out too: add a worker whose job is dividing the work and\n225:   briefing the others.\n226: - **Model choice.** Run every worker on your own model — the one the user\n227:   chose for this conversation — by leaving `model` off `add`. Do not pick other\n228:   models on your own initiative, whether for diversity, cost or quota. The\n229:   exception is the user: if they ask for a different model, for one worker or\n230:   for all of them, use exactly the model they asked for.\n231:   `swarm{action:\"models\"}` lists what you can run, `add{model}` pins it, and\n232:   `swarm{action:\"status\"}` shows what each worker is running. Do not switch a\n233:   member's model, or your own, unless the user asks or the current model is out\n234:   of quota or capacity; say why when you do.\n235: - **Stay lean.** Add a worker when work is queued and nobody is free; kill one\n236:   when there is nothing left for it to pick up (never the narrator or the\n237:   monitor).\n238: - **Fork when context is already warm.** Pass `fork:\"<member>\"` (or\n239:   `\"coordinator\"`) to `add` when a new worker needs the files read, decisions\n240:   made, and reasoning already in that member's conversation instead of starting\n241:   cold — for example when an investigation splits into two branches, or when\n242:   replacing a killed worker whose history is worth keeping.\n243: - **Give authors their own workspace.** Workers share your working copy by\n244:   default. Add authors who edit or build at the same time as others with\n245:   `workspace:\"share\"`: each gets its own working copy of your repository.\n246:   `\"branch\"` is a fully isolated clone. Leave readers and verifiers on\n247:   `inherit`. Have each author commit its own change in its workspace. `kill`\n248:   deletes the workspace, so land the change first: have the author upload it\n249:   for review, or merge or cherry-pick its branch into your working copy. Push\n250:   to a remote only when the user asks. Where a share does not start from your\n251:   working copy as it stands, the `swarm` tool's description says where it\n252:   starts: brief the worker accordingly if its work stacks on yours.\n253:   `status` shows where each worker works.\n254: - **Split a big swarm into channels.** With more than about eight workers, or\n255:   two or more independent work areas, give each area a channel (see\n256:   Channels below) so that its workers are not woken by the other areas' talk.\n257: \n258: ## Channels\n259: \n260: When the `swarm` tool offers the `channel` action, the board can be split into\n261: channels: one per work area (`#parser`, `#ui`). Without it, skip this section:\n262: the board is one channel and everyone sees everything.\n263: \n264: - **Everyone is always in `main`.** A member sees `main` plus the channels it\n265:   is subscribed to; you, the narrator and the monitor see every channel. So\n266:   channels only help when **area work goes in the area's channel and `main`\n267:   carries only what crosses areas**: your decisions, cross-area asks, final\n268:   results.\n269: - **Create, then assign.** `swarm{action:\"channel\", op:\"create\",\n270:   channel:\"parser\", topic:\"lexer and parser\"}`, then\n271:   `swarm{action:\"add\", name, brief, channels:[\"parser\"]}`. The worker's brief\n272:   is posted in its first channel, which is also where its posts go by default.\n273:   A helper a worker adds joins its adder's channels unless `channels` says\n274:   otherwise.\n275: - **Post area briefs and asks in the area.** `post_message{channel:\"parser\",\n276:   text}`. Post to `main` (`channel:\"main\"`) only what the whole swarm needs.\n277: - **`op:\"subscribe\"` / `op:\"unsubscribe\"`** with `channel` and `name` move a\n278:   worker between areas (you may move any worker; a worker may move itself and\n279:   its own helpers). It sees the new channel's posts from then on, not\n280:   earlier ones; tell it to `read_board{channel}` if it needs the history.\n281: - **`op:\"list\"`** shows each channel's topic, creator and subscriber count;\n282:   `status` shows each worker's channels.\n283: - **`op:\"archive\"`** when an area is finished: no more posts, history stays\n284:   readable.\n285: - `read_board{channel:\"parser\"}` reads one channel, `read_board{channel:\"*\"}`\n286:   everything; without `channel` a member reads its own view.\n287: \n288: ## Addressing\n289: \n290: Workers wait to be named: by default a waiting worker wakes only for\n291: `@their-name` (or `@their-prefix` when numbered) or `@all`, and sleeps through\n292: everything else. So:\n293: \n294: - `@name` for work for one worker (or all `<name>-1`..`<name>-<k>` copies in a\n295:   numbered group). This is most posts.\n296: - `@all` for something that is genuinely everyone's: a rule, a decision, an\n297:   interface they all build against. It wakes every worker, so it is not for\n298:   status.\n299: - A post that names nobody is still read — on whatever wakes that worker next —\n300:   but it may be read by nobody for a long time. Put the `@` on.\n301: - **Mentions cross channels.** `@name` reaches its worker whatever channel the\n302:   post is in, and `@all` is still everyone. A worker waiting with\n303:   `mentions_only:false` wakes for anything in its own channels (and `main`),\n304:   not for other channels' posts.\n305: - **`@#channel` calls a whole channel.** `@#parser` wakes every member\n306:   subscribed to `#parser` (and you, the narrator and the monitor), from any\n307:   channel, even ones waiting for mentions only: use it for a cross-area ask\n308:   (\"@#parser is the AST stable?\") instead of `@all`. `@#main` is refused\n309:   (that is `@all`), and an unknown or archived channel calls nobody.\n310: - **`@name` is a wakeup doorbell, never a CC.** Every `@mention` wakes that\n311:   agent and costs it a turn. On peer-to-peer handoffs (`author <-> verifier <->\n312:   submit-shepherd`), instruct workers to `@mention` only the peer(s) who act\n313:   next, and to tag `@coordinator` only when the final deliverable is ready, on\n314:   a blocker or decision, or when no next peer exists.\n315: \n316: Naming a worker gets you its attention, not its agreement.\n317: \n318: ## Waiting\n319: \n320: Call `swarm_wait{since:N}` rather than polling `read_board` in a loop. Waiting\n321: is free: a waiting agent is asleep on the board, costing nothing until somebody\n322: posts. Pass the highest sequence number you have actually seen as `since` — not\n323: the board's head — so that a message posted while you were busy wakes you\n324: instead of being slept through.\n325: \n326: `swarm_wait{mentions_only:false}` wakes you for anything at all. Use it when\n327: you are between decisions and want to see everything; the default, `true`, is\n328: right when you are waiting on one specific answer.\n329: \n330: ## Things that will bite\n331: \n332: - **`kill` is permanent.** A killed worker cannot be revived; replacing it is a\n333:   fresh `add` with a new brief. Do not use `kill` as a pause — a worker that is\n334:   waiting already costs nothing.\n335: - **The board tools arrive one turn late.** `post_message`, `read_board` and\n336:   `swarm_wait` appear on your first turn *after* the first `swarm add`. On the\n337:   turn that creates the swarm, add every worker and end your turn; do not go\n338:   looking for the tools. Brief each worker to post to the next peer in its\n339:   pipeline (or to `@coordinator` when its final deliverable is ready or\n340:   blocked): naming an agent is what wakes it.\n341: - **Workers can add workers, and kill or switch the model of their own.** A\n342:   worker may `swarm{action:\"add\"}` helpers for its own piece (sub-tasks, a\n343:   verifier, a parallel investigation); the helper reports back to that worker,\n344:   which stays responsible for integrating and reporting upward. A worker may\n345:   `kill` only the helpers it added, and `model` itself or the helpers it added;\n346:   you can `kill` any worker and `model` yourself or any worker, and `title` is\n347:   yours alone. Leave a worker's helpers to it: the worker that added them is\n348:   best placed to reuse them or kill them when they are done. Step in only when\n349:   it cannot, for example when you kill a worker, which does not kill the helpers\n350:   it added, so kill any it leaves behind.\n351: - **A new worker starts at the current board head.** Each worker receives its\n352:   scoped brief directly in its first message with its board cursor set past the\n353:   startup briefs so it starts working immediately; if it needs context from an\n354:   earlier board post, point it to that post by sequence number (`#4`).\n355: - **The board outlives the turn.** Everything said is on it, in order, forever;\n356:   `read_board{since:0}` from the beginning if you lose your place.\n357: - **Channels only work if `main` stays quiet.** Area chatter in `main` wakes\n358:   every worker, exactly as if there were no channels. Do not over-split either:\n359:   a channel per worker is no channels at all. Archive an area when it is done.\n360: - **A worker (or you) can run out of quota.** If a worker's turn fails with an error\n361:   showing quota exhaustion (`status` still calls it `waiting`), or a member\n362:   receives a `[quota]` low-quota warning at turn start, switch it to\n363:   another model with `swarm{action:\"model\", name, model}`: it keeps its\n364:   conversation and picks up where it left off on the new model. Any member\n365:   (including you, with `name:\"coordinator\"` or `name` omitted, or a worker\n366:   naming itself) can also switch its own model with `swarm{action:\"model\", model:\"<model>\"}`.\n367:   `swarm{action:\"models\"}` shows live `quota_remaining` and `quota_resets` for\n368:   each model, and `add` and `model` refuse a model whose quota is exhausted\n369:   unless AI credits are paying for it (`models` then says so in `quota_note`).\n370:   When switching a worker, leave `model` off to use your own (since you are\n371:   still running, it is guaranteed not to be out of quota yet), or name the\n372:   model the user asked for. Do not `kill` and re-`add` it. Tell the user which\n373:   model ran out and what you switched to. Switching another worker only works\n374:   when it is `waiting`, so wait for one that is `working` to stop first.\n375: - **Nothing restarts on its own after quota runs out.** A member whose turn\n376:   failed on quota sits out until the board moves, its model is switched, or the\n377:   user messages it. When the user tells you to continue, switch each member\n378:   that ran out (the error messages you got name them), or, if quota is back or\n379:   AI credits now cover it, post to each by name so it picks up its work."}
{"step_index":53,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-18T04:52:53Z","input_tokens":9253,"cache_read_tokens":236650,"output_tokens":6171,"thinking":"I'm splitting the work so each piece has its own files, since v5_env.py is the one most likely to collide. The env worker takes ownership of the scene builder and handles the origin bug, the fence posts, the masts, brake poles, merged truss beams, pit box lines, and the empty collection cleanup, then rebuilds env and anim and checks frames.\n\nI'm weighing how to divide the remaining work so the tidy and verifier steps wait on the env worker's results. The docs and archive cleanup should only happen once env has confirmed its output, and I'm considering whether the clay and color re-renders are worth their long run times given the clay video is broken.\n\nNext I'm finishing the brief for the env worker: it needs the Blender path, the bug locations, and the rule that the clay re-render only starts after the verifier passes, while I leave the color re-render for the user to decide.\n\nI'm settling the env worker's fix for the scene builder: refresh the view layer before reading world matrices, and only re-origin meshes whose location is zero. For assembly parenting, I'm weighing empties named under the existing V5_Stand_ prefix so they group with their stands, keeping children's transforms intact.\n\nI'm settling on merged per-stand meshes for the repetitive small parts, like the truss, columns, and flags with their poles, while keeping the larger pieces such as the tier blocks, back, roof, and VIP separate. The pit box lines would collapse into a single mesh, and I'm still working out how the brake board poles should be handled.\n\nI'm planning the verifier's role: it will work read-only, running the audit scripts from its own scratch folder, and I'm considering pointing it at a dedicated path under the brain directory.\n\nNext I'm sorting out the handoffs: the verifier checks the env worker's output using the existing audit scripts, and only after a pass does the env worker re-render the clay video and the tidy worker finish the docs and cleanup.\n\nI'm finishing the env worker's brief and deciding that it owns v5_anim.py too, so the anim edits don't collide with a rebuild. The tidy worker stays small, handling docs and file cleanup. Next I'll check the workspace setting, which should inherit, and then write the briefs.\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"swarm","args":{"action":"title","title":"Silverstone V5 scene cleanup"}},{"name":"swarm","args":{"action":"add","brief":"You own the scene build for the Blender project at d:\\项目session\\blender-supercar: **pipeline/v5_env.py** and **pipeline/v5_anim.py**. Nobody else edits these two files. The user wants: remove redundant models, keep the scene very easy for a human to edit, and keep improving it. Read README.md and docs/README_V5.md first.\n\nBlender 5.3 (CPU only, EEVEE): `D:\\APPs\\steam\\steamapps\\common\\Blender\\blender.exe -b --factory-startup -P <script>`. Pipeline: v5_env.py (reads pipeline/v4_stage_rig.blend → writes LATEST/v5_stage_env.blend, about 3 min) → v5_anim.py (→ LATEST/v5_stage_anim.blend, about 1 min) → v5_clay.py (→ LATEST/v5_stage_anim_clay.blend). The shell is PowerShell and prints a big ASCII banner first, which you can ignore.\n\nProblems already confirmed (fix all of them):\n1. **CRITICAL: 441 of 672 V5_ objects are piled at world (0,0,0)** in the current LATEST/v5_stage_env.blend, v5_stage_anim.blend and the clay file. The cause is step 13 of v5_env.py (around lines 1059-1072). It reads `ob.matrix_world` on objects that were just created and never got a depsgraph update, so every `box()`/sphere object (they set `ob.location`) has an identity matrix_world, and the code sets `ob.location = (0,0,0)`. That hits ad boards, stand columns/trusses/posts/back/VIP/roof strips, the gantry, bridges, screens, marshal posts, hangars, the pit garage, start lights and brake boards. The clay video renders_v5/f1_silverstone_clay_1080p.mp4 is broken because of this (renders_v5/clay_frames/f_0001.jpg is a wall of boxes). Fix it with `bpy.context.view_layer.update()` before reading matrix_world, and only re-origin objects whose mesh is baked in world space (location == 0).\n2. `V5_FencePosts` (around lines 623-638): the `for dx, dy` loop ignores dx/dy, so all 972 faces have zero area and are invisible. Offset ±0.07 m along the track tangent so they are real thin posts.\n3. `V5_FieldMasts` (around lines 1020-1037): the pole faces use absolute indices `(0,1,5,4)…` instead of `base+…`. The result is 44 duplicate faces on mast 0, 11 lamp heads floating with no poles, and 88 loose verts. Offset by base.\n4. `brake_board` (around lines 896-902): the `for d in (-0.32, 0.32)` loop ignores d, so 18 duplicate pole objects (`*_pole` / `*_pole.001`) sit in the same spot. Offset the poles by d along the tangent.\n5. Redundant tiny objects: each stand's `_Truss{i}` / `_TrussB{i}` are 0.16 m cubes (about 172 objects, invisible at distance, meant to be \"transverse steel beams\"). Replace them with real beams from the front roof edge to the back roof edge, merged into **one mesh per stand**. Also merge the repeated small parts of each stand (Col*, Flagpole*, V5_Flag_* triangles; keep their materials as a multi-material mesh) into one mesh per kind per stand. Merge the 60 `V5_PitBoxLine_*` quads into one mesh. Keep big or individually meaningful parts separate: tier blocks, back wall, roof, VIP, each ad board, kerbs, etc.\n6. Editability: give every assembly an Empty parent placed at its center, and parent the parts with keep-transform. One Empty per stand (name it e.g. `V5_Stand_Main` so the existing prefix bucketing still files it under V5_04_Stands), per bridge, per big screen, per brake-board group, per marshal post, and for the gantry + start lights. Moving the Empty must move the whole assembly. Remove the empty clutter collections `09_Studio_Stage_Rig` and `WheelSpokes_*` (they hold 0 objects). Do not change the car meshes or the rig.\n7. v5_anim.py: delete the duplicated first definitions of `CAR_LEN`, `SENSOR_W` and `lens_for_framing` (around lines 379-389). Behaviour must not change. Keep the physics self-check output PASS.\n\nApart from the bug fixes above, the render must not change in look: same materials, lighting, cameras and animation. Then rebuild env → anim → clay. Render test stills at frames 40,700,1500 (`v5_anim.py -- --test 40,700,1500` writes to renders_v5/test/) and compare them with the matching renders_v5/frames/f_XXXX.jpg (the old good color renders). Report object count before/after (919 env objects before).\n\nWhen done, post to @verifier with: what changed, file paths, object counts and test-still paths. Fix whatever the verifier sends back. After verifier PASS, re-render the clay version (`pipeline/render_clay.py`, about 12 min; delete the old renders_v5/clay_frames/*.jpg first, because the script skips existing frames) and run `bash pipeline/mux_clay.sh` (Git Bash). Then post the final summary to @tidy and @coordinator. Do not re-render the color film.","name":"env"}},{"name":"swarm","args":{"action":"add","brief":"You are the verifier for the Blender project at d:\\项目session\\blender-supercar. Worker `env` is fixing pipeline/v5_env.py and v5_anim.py and rebuilding LATEST/v5_stage_env.blend, v5_stage_anim.blend and v5_stage_anim_clay.blend. You report problems; you do not edit project files. Write any scripts you need under C:\\Users\\86052\\.gemini\\antigravity\\brain\\16b5f3cb-4bce-43d1-a09a-ebf98bd665b7\\scratch\\ . Existing helpers there: audit_scene.py (heavy meshes, loose verts, bbox overlap groups, images) and check_origin_pile.py (counts V5_ meshes whose world bbox center is near (0,0,0)). Blender: `D:\\APPs\\steam\\steamapps\\common\\Blender\\blender.exe -b --factory-startup -P <script>` (PowerShell; ignore the ASCII banner).\n\nBaseline before the fix: 919 objects in env and 948 in anim; 441 of 672 V5_ meshes piled at world origin (the critical bug); V5_FencePosts all zero-area faces; V5_FieldMasts with duplicate faces and 88 loose verts; 18 duplicate brake-board pole objects; about 172 tiny 0.16 m truss cubes. LATEST/v5_stage_env.blend1 (dated 11/14) is an older build with correct placement; use it as a placement reference.\n\nWait until @env posts that the rebuild is ready, then check:\n1. No V5_ objects are piled at the origin. Assemblies sit where they did in the 11/14 reference: compare world bbox centers of stands, ad boards, garage, bridges, screens, hangars.\n2. Fence posts and field masts are real geometry; no duplicate co-located objects or duplicate faces; no loose verts in V5_ meshes.\n3. Editability: each assembly has an Empty parent at its center, and moving it moves all its children (test by translating the Empty in a script and checking children world positions). Collections V5_01..V5_07 are intact. Origins of individual objects are at their geometry, not 1 km away. The empty collections 09_Studio_Stage_Rig and WheelSpokes_* are gone.\n4. Car meshes, rig, 26 cameras and timeline markers, the 2161-frame range and materials are unchanged. The v5_anim physics self-check prints PASS.\n5. Look at env's test stills in renders_v5/test/ next to renders_v5/frames/f_0040.jpg, f_0700.jpg and f_1500.jpg: same look, no stray boxes.\n\nPost PASS or a numbered list of concrete defects to @env. On final PASS also tag @coordinator with a short verdict.","name":"verifier"}},{"name":"swarm","args":{"action":"add","brief":"You own project docs and file housekeeping for the Blender project at d:\\项目session\\blender-supercar: README.md, LATEST/README.txt, docs/README_V5.md, and moving or deleting redundant files. Do NOT edit pipeline/v5_env.py or pipeline/v5_anim.py (worker `env` owns them) and do not touch the .blend files while env is rebuilding.\n\nStart now (safe in parallel):\n1. pipeline/v5_organize.py is superseded by step 13 of v5_env.py. Move it to archive/scripts/ (create a v5 subfolder) and remove references to it in README.md.\n2. Fix stale facts in docs/README_V5.md: section 7 lists `pipeline/v5_stage_env.blend` / `pipeline/v5_stage_anim.blend`, but they live in LATEST/. Several places say \"25 机位\" but there are 26 cameras. Section 7 does not mention the clay files (v5_clay.py, render_clay.py, mux_clay.sh, LATEST/v5_stage_anim_clay.blend) or v5_viewport_fast.py. README.md line 9 says LATEST holds only two files, but it also holds the clay blend.\n3. Draft (do not yet write the final numbers) a short \"编辑指南 / editing guide\" section for README.md and LATEST/README.txt. It should explain that each stand, bridge, screen, brake-board group, marshal post and the gantry has an Empty parent, so you select the Empty and press G/R to move the whole assembly; that repeated small parts are merged per stand; and the collection list V5_01..V5_07.\n\nWait for @env's final summary (it comes after the verifier passes). Then:\n4. Update the docs with the real object counts and what changed, plus a section-9-style entry in README_V5.md for the \"441 objects piled at origin\" bug (cause: matrix_world read before depsgraph update; fix: view_layer.update()).\n5. Delete redundant model files: LATEST/*.blend1, pipeline/v4_stage_rig.blend1, and the stale copy _unrelated/新建文件夹/v5_stage_env.blend. Before deleting, confirm that LATEST/v5_stage_env.blend, v5_stage_anim.blend and v5_stage_anim_clay.blend all exist and are newer. Keep pipeline/v4_stage_rig.blend (it is the pipeline input) and everything in archive/.\nPost a list of changed, moved and deleted files to @coordinator.","name":"tidy"}},{"name":"swarm","args":{"action":"add","brief":"You are this swarm's stall monitor. Load the swarm_monitor skill and follow it. Post only to break a stall.","name":"monitor"}}]}
